netlink: report the peer of a veth in IFLA_LINK

What: an RTM_NEWLINK message for one end of a veth pair, in a dump, a reply or
a notification, holds the index of the other end in IFLA_LINK, its index in
its own network namespace, as Linux's rtnl_fill_ifinfo() puts
dev_get_iflink(), which drivers/net/veth.c:veth_get_iflink() gives as the
peer's ifindex. As in Linux, it is left out when it equals the interface's own
index and the peer is in the same namespace; a peer in another namespace is
put whatever its index, as rtnl_fill_link_netnsid() has nla_put_iflink() do.
To find the peer's NIC, the veth endpoint and the nested link endpoints that
wrap it tell the dispatcher they are attached to, and a NIC, and a bridge
port, which is a NIC's dispatcher while the NIC is part of a bridge, tell
their NIC's ID and stack.

Why: netavark creates the veth pair with the container end in the container's
namespace, reads the container end back, and brings the host end up by the
index in its IFLA_LINK (src/network/bridge.rs, create_veth_pair(), in 1.4.0,
1.14.0 and 1.17.2). Without it, it asked for interface 0: "netavark: failed
to set host veth up: Netlink error: Invalid argument (os error 22)".

Not done: Linux also puts IFLA_LINK_NETNSID when the peer is in another
namespace; gVisor has no namespace IDs, so `ip link` there names the peer
after the interface of its index in its own namespace (veth@eth0 rather than
veth@if2). A veth whose peer is gone is not reported with IFLA_LINK 0.

Upstream: none found; drop this patch when upstream reports IFLA_LINK.

Verified:
- The new syscall test NetlinkRouteTest.VethLink (a pair in one namespace,
  after one end joins a bridge, a pair across namespaces, and one across two
  new namespaces whose ends both have index 2) passes on
  Linux 7.0 under runc and on runsc with this patch, under the runtime
  arguments of runsc and of runsc-nested, and fails on ALE's 0.1.2. The unit
  tests of //pkg/tcpip/link/veth, //pkg/tcpip/link/nested, //pkg/tcpip/stack
  (stack_test, stack_x_test, bridge_test) and
  //pkg/sentry/socket/netstack pass.
- netavark 1.4.0, 1.14.0 and 1.17.2 brought the host end of the pair up and
  attached the container to its bridge; the containers reached the Internet
  once patch 0016 gave netavark its sysctls.

